@3.4 Strings
.
=3.4 STRINGS
.
=WHAT IT COSTS
.
.  ASM: GP.INSTR GP.STRPTR GP.COMP
.    ASM -- a keyword, run by machine code in the GP runtime block. A program
.    using ANY GP keyword pays for the whole block.
.  COMPOSITE: GP.CONTAINS GP.ISEMPTY
.    COMPOSITE -- a keyword with no machine code of its own. The compiler
.    expands it into keywords that already exist, so it costs the runtime
.    nothing at all.
.
=DESCRIPTION
.
.  GP.INSTR(hay$, needle$ [,start])
.      DOES: position of needle$, 1-based; 0 = not found. start is where to
.        begin
.
.  GP.CONTAINS(hay$, needle$)
.      DOES: -1 if needle$ occurs anywhere in hay$, 0 if not. Composite --
.        see below
.
.  GP.ISEMPTY(a$)
.      DOES: -1 if a$ has zero length, 0 if not. Composite
.
.  GP.COMP(a$, b$)
.      DOES: compare ignoring case: -1 before, 0 same, 1 after
.
.  GP.STRPTR(a$)
.      DOES: address of the string's [ActLen][Data] block
.
.Trimming and case folding are in STRCASE.INC.BL (SEC 4.8) rather than
.keywords: they would take 188 bytes of the all-or-nothing GP block to serve
.one caller in the tree outside their own example. GP.STRPTR is a keyword
.because the module is built on it.
.
.GP.INSTR is the only string search GPC has; without it there is none.
.
.GP.CONTAINS answers the same question without the position: IF
.GP.CONTAINS(F$, ".BAS"). It is case sensitive, comparing raw bytes as
.GP.INSTR does; use GP.COMP for a case-blind test. An empty needle returns 0,
.not -1, because GP.INSTR reports not-found for one.
.
.GP.CONTAINS is composite. The compiler expands it to GP.INSTR(hay$, needle$)
.<> 0, and the object is byte-for-byte identical to writing that out
.(verified by compiling both). Use whichever spelling reads better; there is
.no size difference.
.
.GP.ISEMPTY(a$) is LEN(a$) = 0. A string of spaces is not empty -- apply
.STRCASE.TRIM first if that is the intent. It compiles to the same four bytes
.as LEN(a$)=0, and IF a$="" costs about the same (a literal points into the
.p-code, so the empty string allocates nothing). The three spellings do not
.differ in size.
.
.GP.COMP provides a case-blind equality test, IF GP.COMP(A$,B$) = 0, which =
.cannot do, and an ordering comparator. Length breaks a tie: "abc" sorts
.before "ABCD".
.
.The in-place statements take a string variable, never a literal or an
.expression; the compiler rejects those. Case conversion leaves digits,
.punctuation and PETSCII graphics unchanged.
.
.There is no GP.PAD. Padding grows a string, and an in-place handler receives
.the string block and not the variable slot, so it cannot reallocate beyond
.the capacity the string was created with. Use STR.PADR / PADL / PADC (SEC
.4.2), which are BASIC assignments and do reallocate.
.
=GP.STRPTR and the address-splitting trap
.
.The address is that of [ActLen][Data]: the length byte is at the address,
.the first character at +1, and the block capacity at -2. With GP.CALL,
.machine code can fill a BASIC string in place and set its length, which
.stock BASIC cannot do.
.
.Split the address with GP.LOBYTE / GP.HIBYTE, not with P AND 255:
.
:GP.CALL $A000, GP.LOBYTE(P), GP.HIBYTE(P)
.
.AND is 16-bit signed and the string heap is above 32,767, so P AND 255
.raises OUT OF RANGE. The longhand H = INT(P / 256) : L = P - H * 256 is
.correct and is what the keywords compile to. The same applies to GP.ARRPTR
.and to any VRAM address in the top eighth of memory.
.
=EXAMPLE PROGRAM
.
.  STRINGS.EXP.BL -- runnable, in the GPC-BASIC folder
.
=SEE ALSO
.
